we've covered upload speed hiccups for  large files but what about when it's  time to read on this episode of cloud  storage bytes you'll need your reading  glasses because we're talking large file  downloads we've all been there staring  at our watch wondering how much longer  it's gonna take for that large file to  move from cloud storage to the virtual  machine where computation occurs even if  it's only two minutes that's an eternity  as far as we're concerned especially  when we're paying for that computing  power during our download ouch when  looking at our options here we might  recall that gsutil saved the day for our  uploads but that doesn't seem to be the  case for our downloads we even found  that curl outperforms GSU till here but  why and just when we thought we had it  all figured out luckily I'm on the case  and as it turns out the default settings  for GS util don't work particularly well  with large file downloads this is  because these default settings are  designed to spread downloads across four  threads using one process we should be  able to improve the performance by  limiting the number of threads and  forcing the system to use multiple  processes instead for a quick recap  processes are instances of execution  while threads are individual executions  that run inside of a process we want  more processes but each focused on only  one task  so first up limiting the number of  threads to do this there's a  command-line option to specify the  thread count we'll go ahead and reduce  it to one with this small change we've  improved the performance but we're still  not at the level that curl is showing  when we're fetching through HTTP so next  up forcing multiple processes to do this  we're going to have a throwback moment  to a previous cloud storage bytes where  we discussed a parallel composite upload  now it's time for parallel or sliced  downloads which we can trigger by  telling GS util to use a certain number  of components so to recap decreasing the  number of threads and increasing the  number of components can work together  to dramatically improve your download  speed there's plenty more to come about  optimizing performance but that's for  another episode  if you want to know more about  downloading large files check out the  links in the description and as always  don't forget to subscribe give us a like  and tell us what you want to learn about  Google Cloud storage thanks for joining  us for this quick byte of cloud storage  see you next time  [Music]
 

